485→        root_path (str): The initial portion of the request URL's \"path\" that\n   486→            corresponds to the application object, so that the application\n   487→            knows its virtual \"location\". This defaults to the empty string,\n   488→            indicating that the application corresponds to the \"root\" of the\n   489→            server.\n   490→        protocol: The protocol to use for the URL scheme\n   491→            (default: 'http')\n   492→        port (int): The TCP port to simulate. Defaults to\n   493→            the standard port used by the given scheme (i.e., 80 for 'http'\n   494→            and 443 for 'https'). A string may also be passed, as long as\n   495→            it can be parsed as an int.\n   496→        params (dict): A dictionary of query string parameters,\n   497→            where each key is a parameter name, and each value is\n   498→            either a ``str`` or something that can be converted\n   499→            into a ``str``, or a list of such values. If a ``list``,\n   500→            the value will be converted to a comma-delimited string\n   501→            of values (e.g., 'thing=1,2,3').\n   502→        params_csv (bool): Set to ``True`` to encode list values\n   503→            in query string params as comma-separated values\n   504→            (e.g., 'thing=1,2,3'). Otherwise, parameters will be encoded by\n   505→            specifying multiple instances of the parameter\n   506→            (e.g., 'thing=1&thing=2&thing=3'). Defaults to ``False``.\n   507→        query_string (str): A raw query string to include in the\n   508→            request (default: ``None``). If specified, overrides\n   509→            `params`.\n   510→        content_type (str): The value to use for the Content-Type header in\n   511→            the request. If specified, this value will take precedence over\n   512→            any value set for the Content-Type header in the\n   513→            `headers` keyword ar