Upon\n   922→    entering and exiting the context, the appropriate ASGI lifespan events\n   923→    will be simulated.\n   924→\n   925→    Within the context, HTTP requests can be simulated using an interface\n   926→    that is similar to :class:`~.TestClient`, except that all ``simulate_*()``\n   927→    methods are coroutines::\n   928→\n   929→        async with testing.ASGIConductor(some_app) as conductor:\n   930→            async def post_events():\n   931→                for i in range(100):\n   932→                    await conductor.simulate_post('/events', json={'id': i}):\n   933→                    await asyncio.sleep(0.01)\n   934→\n   935→            async def get_events_sse():\n   936→                # Here, we will get only some of the single server-sent events\n   937→                # because the non-streaming method is \"single-shot\". In other\n   938→                # words, simulate_get() will emit a client disconnect event\n   939→                # into the app before returning.\n   940→                result = await conductor.simulate_get('/events')\n   941→\n   942→                # Alternatively, we can use simulate_get_stream() as a context\n   943→                # manager to perform a series of reads on the result body that\n   944→                # are interleaved with the execution of the post_events()\n   945→                # coroutine.\n   946→                async with conductor.simulate_get_stream('/events') as sr:\n   947→                    while some_condition:\n   948→                        # Read next body chunk that was received (if any).\n   949→                        chunk = await sr.stream.read()\n   950→\n   951→                        if chunk:\n   952→                            # TODO: Do something with the chunk\n   953→                            pass\n   954→\n   955→                    # Exiting the context causes the request event emitter to\n   956→                    # begin emitting ``'http.disconnect'`` e