eters,\n  1736→            where each key is a parameter name, and each value is\n  1737→            either a ``str`` or something that can be converted\n  1738→            into a ``str``, or a list of such values. If a ``list``,\n  1739→            the value will be converted to a comma-delimited string\n  1740→            of values (e.g., 'thing=1,2,3').\n  1741→        params_csv (bool): Set to ``True`` to encode list values\n  1742→            in query string params as comma-separated values\n  1743→            (e.g., 'thing=1,2,3'). Otherwise, parameters will be encoded by\n  1744→            specifying multiple instances of the parameter\n  1745→            (e.g., 'thing=1&thing=2&thing=3'). Defaults to ``False``.\n  1746→        query_string (str): A raw query string to include in the\n  1747→            request (default: ``None``). If specified, overrides\n  1748→            `params`.\n  1749→        content_type (str): The value to use for the Content-Type header in\n  1750→            the request. If specified, this value will take precedence over\n  1751→            any value set for the Content-Type header in the\n  1752→            `headers` keyword argument. The ``falcon`` module provides a number\n  1753→            of :ref:`constants for common media types <media_type_constants>`.\n  1754→        headers (dict): Extra headers as a dict-like (Mapping) object, or an\n  1755→            iterable yielding a series of two-member (*name*, *value*)\n  1756→            iterables. Each pair of strings provides the name and value\n  1757→            for an HTTP header. If desired, multiple header values may be\n  1758→            combined into a single (*name*, *value*) pair by joining the values\n  1759→            with a comma when the header in question supports the list\n  1760→            format (see also RFC 7230 and RFC 7231). Header names are not\n  1761→            case-sensitive.\n  1762→\n  1763→            Note:\n  1764→                If a User-Ag