e converted\n   499\u2192            into a ``str``, or a list of such values. If a ``list``,\n   500\u2192            the value will be converted to a comma-delimited string\n   501\u2192            of values (e.g., 'thing=1,2,3').\n   502\u2192        params_csv (bool): Set to ``True`` to encode list values\n   503\u2192            in query string params as comma-separated values\n   504\u2192            (e.g., 'thing=1,2,3'). Otherwise, parameters will be encoded by\n   505\u2192            specifying multiple instances of the parameter\n   506\u2192            (e.g., 'thing=1&thing=2&thing=3'). Defaults to ``False``.\n   507\u2192        query_string (str): A raw query string to include in the\n   508\u2192            request (default: ``None``). If specified, overrides\n   509\u2192            `params`.\n   510\u2192        content_type (str): The value to use for the Content-Type header in\n   511\u2192            the request. If specified, this value will take precedence over\n   512\u2192            any value set for the Content-Type header in the\n   513\u2192            `headers` keyword argument. The ``falcon`` module provides a number\n   514\u2192            of :ref:`constants for common media types <media_type_constants>`.\n   515\u2192        headers (dict): Extra headers as a dict-like (Mapping) object, or an\n   516\u2192            iterable yielding a series of two-member (*name*, *value*)\n   517\u2192            iterables. Each pair of strings provides the name and value\n   518\u2192            for an HTTP header. If desired, multiple header values may be\n   519\u2192            combined into a single (*name*, *value*) pair by joining the values\n   520\u2192            with a comma when the header in question supports the list\n   521\u2192            format (see also RFC 7230 and RFC 7231). Header names are not\n   522\u2192            case-sensitive.\n   523\u2192\n   524\u2192            Note:\n   525\u2192                If a User-Agent header is not provided, it will default to::\n   526\u2192\n   527\u2192                    f'falcon-client/{falcon.__version__}'\n   528\u2192\n   529\u2192        body (str): The body of the request (default ''). The value will be\n   530\u2192            encoded as UTF-8 in the WSGI environ. Alternatively, a byte string\n   531\u2192            may be passed, in which case it will be used as-is.\n   532\u2192        json(JSON serializable): A JSON document to serialize as the\n   533\u2192            body of the request (default: ``None``). If specified,\n   534\u2192            overrides `body` and sets the Content-Type header to\n   535\u2192            ``'application/json'``, overriding any value specified by either\n   536\u2192            the `content_type` or `headers` arguments.\n   537\u2192        file_wrapper (callable): Callable that returns an iterable,\n   538\u2192            to be used as the value for *wsgi.file_wrapper* in the\n   539\u2192            WSGI environ (default: ``None``). This can be used to test\n   540\u2192            high-performance file transmission when `resp.stream` is\n   541\u2192            set to a file-like object.\n   542\u2192        host(str): A string to use for the hostname part of the fully\n   543\u2192            qualified request URL (default: 'falconframework.org')\n   544\u2192        remote_addr (str): A string to use as the remote IP address for the\n   545\u2192            request (default: '127.0.0.1'). For WSGI, this corresponds to\n   546\u2192            the 'REMOTE_ADDR' environ variable. For ASGI, this corresponds\n   547\u2192            to the IP address used for the 'client' field in the connection\n   548\u2192            scope.\n   549\u2192        http_version (str): The HTTP version to simulate. Must be either\n   550\u2192            '2', '2.0', 1.1', '1.0', or '1' (default '1.1'). If set to '1.0',\n   551\u2192            the Host header will not be added to the scope.\n   552\u2192        wsgi