le server-sent events\n   937\u2192                # because the non-streaming method is \"single-shot\". In other\n   938\u2192                # words, simulate_get() will emit a client disconnect event\n   939\u2192                # into the app before returning.\n   940\u2192                result = await conductor.simulate_get('/events')\n   941\u2192\n   942\u2192                # Alternatively, we can use simulate_get_stream() as a context\n   943\u2192                # manager to perform a series of reads on the result body that\n   944\u2192                # are interleaved with the execution of the post_events()\n   945\u2192                # coroutine.\n   946\u2192                async with conductor.simulate_get_stream('/events') as sr:\n   947\u2192                    while some_condition:\n   948\u2192                        # Read next body chunk that was received (if any).\n   949\u2192                        chunk = await sr.stream.read()\n   950\u2192\n   951\u2192                        if chunk:\n   952\u2192                            # TODO: Do something with the chunk\n   953\u2192                            pass\n   954\u2192\n   955\u2192                    # Exiting the context causes the request event emitter to\n   956\u2192                    # begin emitting ``'http.disconnect'`` events and then awaits\n   957\u2192                    # the completion of the asyncio task that is running the\n   958\u2192                    # simulated ASGI request.\n   959\u2192\n   960\u2192            asyncio.gather(post_events(), get_events_sse())\n   961\u2192\n   962\u2192    Note:\n   963\u2192        Because the :class:`~.ASGIConductor` interface uses coroutines,\n   964\u2192        it cannot be used directly with synchronous testing frameworks such as\n   965\u2192        pytest.\n   966\u2192\n   967\u2192        As a workaround, the test can be adapted by wrapping it in\n   968\u2192        an inline async function and then invoking it via\n   969\u2192        :meth:`falcon.async_to_sync` or decorating the test function\n   970\u2192        with :meth:`falcon.runs_sync`.\n   971\u2192\n   972\u2192        Alternatively, you can try searching PyPI to see if an async plugin is\n   973\u2192        available for your testing framework of choice. For example, the\n   974\u2192        ``pytest-asyncio`` plugin is available for ``pytest`` users.\n   975\u2192\n   976\u2192    Similar to the :class:`TestClient`, :class:`ASGIConductor` also exposes\n   977\u2192    convenience aliases without the ``simulate_`` prefix. Just as with a\n   978\u2192    typical asynchronous HTTP client, it is possible to simply invoke::\n   979\u2192\n   980\u2192        await conductor.get('/messages')\n   981\u2192        await conductor.request('LOCK', '/files/first')\n   982\u2192\n   983\u2192    Args:\n   984\u2192        app (callable): An ASGI application to target when simulating\n   985\u2192            requests.\n   986\u2192\n   987\u2192    Keyword Arguments:\n   988\u2192        headers (dict): Default headers to set on every request (default\n   989\u2192            ``None``). These defaults may be overridden by passing values\n   990\u2192            for the same headers to one of the ``simulate_*()`` methods.\n   991\u2192\n   992\u2192    Attributes:\n   993\u2192        app: The app that this client instance was configured to use.\n   994\u2192\n   995\u2192    \"\"\"\n   996\u2192\n   997\u2192    def __init__(self, app, headers=None):\n   998\u2192        if not _is_asgi_app(app):\n   999\u2192            raise CompatibilityError('ASGIConductor may only be used with an ASGI app')\n  1000\u2192\n  1001\u2192        self.app = app\n  1002\u2192        self._default_headers = headers\n  1003\u2192\n  1004\u2192        self._shutting_down = asyncio.Condition()\n  1005\u2192        self._lifespan_event_collector = helpers.ASGIResponseEventCollector()\n  1006\u2192        self._lifespan_task = None\n  1007\u2192\n  1