d must be an awaitable coroutine function '\n   743\u2192                    'in order to be used safely with an ASGI app.'\n   744\u2192                )\n   745\u2192\n   746\u2192        super().add_sink(sink, prefix=prefix)\n   747\u2192\n   748\u2192    add_sink.__doc__ = falcon.app.App.add_sink.__doc__\n   749\u2192\n   750\u2192    def add_error_handler(\n   751\u2192        self,\n   752\u2192        exception: Union[Type[BaseException], Iterable[Type[BaseException]]],\n   753\u2192        handler: Optional[ErrorHandler] = None,\n   754\u2192    ):\n   755\u2192        \"\"\"Register a handler for one or more exception types.\n   756\u2192\n   757\u2192        Error handlers may be registered for any exception type, including\n   758\u2192        :class:`~.HTTPError` or :class:`~.HTTPStatus`. This feature\n   759\u2192        provides a central location for logging and otherwise handling\n   760\u2192        exceptions raised by responders, hooks, and middleware components.\n   761\u2192\n   762\u2192        A handler can raise an instance of :class:`~.HTTPError` or\n   763\u2192        :class:`~.HTTPStatus` to communicate information about the issue to\n   764\u2192        the client.  Alternatively, a handler may modify `resp`\n   765\u2192        directly.\n   766\u2192\n   767\u2192        An error handler \"matches\" a raised exception if the exception is an\n   768\u2192        instance of the corresponding exception type. If more than one error\n   769\u2192        handler matches the raised exception, the framework will choose the\n   770\u2192        most specific one, as determined by the method resolution order of the\n   771\u2192        raised exception type. If multiple error handlers are registered for the\n   772\u2192        *same* exception class, then the most recently-registered handler is\n   773\u2192        used.\n   774\u2192\n   775\u2192        For example, suppose we register error handlers as follows::\n   776\u2192\n   777\u2192            app = App()\n   778\u2192            app.add_error_handler(falcon.HTTPNotFound, custom_handle_not_found)\n   779\u2192            app.add_error_handler(falcon.HTTPError, custom_handle_http_error)\n   780\u2192            app.add_error_handler(Exception, custom_handle_uncaught_exception)\n   781\u2192            app.add_error_handler(falcon.HTTPNotFound, custom_handle_404)\n   782\u2192\n   783\u2192        If an instance of ``falcon.HTTPForbidden`` is raised, it will be\n   784\u2192        handled by ``custom_handle_http_error()``. ``falcon.HTTPError`` is a\n   785\u2192        superclass of ``falcon.HTTPForbidden`` and a subclass of ``Exception``,\n   786\u2192        so it is the most specific exception type with a registered handler.\n   787\u2192\n   788\u2192        If an instance of ``falcon.HTTPNotFound`` is raised, it will be handled\n   789\u2192        by ``custom_handle_404()``, not by ``custom_handle_not_found()``, because\n   790\u2192        ``custom_handle_404()`` was registered more recently.\n   791\u2192\n   792\u2192        Note:\n   793\u2192\n   794\u2192            By default, the framework installs three handlers, one for\n   795\u2192            :class:`~.HTTPError`, one for :class:`~.HTTPStatus`, and one for\n   796\u2192            the standard ``Exception`` type, which prevents passing uncaught\n   797\u2192            exceptions to the WSGI server. These can be overridden by adding a\n   798\u2192            custom error handler method for the exception type in question.\n   799\u2192\n   800\u2192            When a generic unhandled exception is raised while\n   801\u2192            handling a :ref:`WebSocket <ws>` connection, the default handler will\n   802\u2192            close the connection with the standard close code ``1011`` (Internal\n   803\u2192            Error). If your ASGI server does not support this code, the\n   804\u2192            framework will use code ``3011`` instead; or you can customize\n   805\u2192            it via the\n   806\u2192            :attr:`~falcon.asgi.WebSocketOptions.error_close_code`\n   807\u2192            property of :attr:`~.ws_options`.\n   808\u2192\n   809\u2192            On the other hand, if an ``on_websocket()`` responder raises an\n   810\u2192            instance of :class:`~falcon.HTTPError`, the default error handler\n   811\u2192            will close the :ref:`WebSocket <ws>` connection with a framework\n   812\u2192            close code derived by adding ``3000`` to the HTTP status code (e.g.,\n   813\u2192            ``3404``)\n   814\u2192\n   815\u2192        Args:\n   816\u2192            exception (type or iterable of types): When handling a request,\n   817\u2192                whenever an error occurs that is an instance of the specified\n   818\u2192                type(s), the associated handler will be called. Either a single\n   819\u2192                type or an iterable of types may be specified.\n   820\u2192\n   821\u2192        Keyword Args:\n   822\u2192            handler (callable): A coroutine function taking the\n   823\u2192                form::\n   824\u2192\n   825\u2192                    async def func(req, resp, ex, params, ws=None):\n   826\u2192                        pass\n   827\u2192\n   828\u2192                In the case of a WebSocket connection, the `resp` argument\n   829\u2192                will be ``None``, while the `ws` keyword argument\n   830\u2192                will receive the :class:`~falcon.asgi.WebSocket` object\n   831\u2192                representing the connection.\n   832\u2192\n   833\u2192                If the `handler` keyword argument is not provided to\n   834\u2192                :meth:`~.add_error_handler`, the handler will default to\n   835\u2192                ``exception.handle``, where ``exception`` is the error type\n   836\u2192                specified above, and ``handle`` is a static method (i.e.,\n   837\u2192                decorated with ``@static